{"name":"Blueberry Mist","hex":"#cbccdf","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"blueberry","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An edible round berry, belonging to the cowberry group (Vaccinium sect. Cyanococcus), with flared crowns at the end, that turns blue on ripening.","The shrub of the above-mentioned berry."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a dark blue colour.","Resembling or characteristic of blueberries."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blueberry"},{"term":"mist","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Water or other liquid finely suspended in air.","A layer of fine droplets or particles."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To form mist.","To spray fine droplets on, particularly of water."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mist"}],"entries":[{"title":"Blueberry","blocks":[{"text":"Blueberries are a widely distributed and widespread group of perennial flowering plants with blue or purple berries. They are classified in the section Cyanococcus within the genus Vaccinium. Commercial blueberries—both wild (lowbush) and cultivated (highbush)—are all native to North America. The highbush varieties were introduced into Europe during the 1930s."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/Blueberries.jpg/330px-Blueberries.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blueberry","wikidata":"Q13178"},{"title":"Mist","blocks":[{"text":"Mist is a natural phenomenon caused by small droplets of water aerosols suspended in the cold air, usually by condensation. Physically, it is an example of a dispersion, most commonly seen where water vapor in warm, moist air meets sudden cooling, such as in exhaled air in the winter, or when hot sauna steam is suddenly released outside. Mist occurs naturally as part of weather, typically when humid air comes into contact with surfaces that are much cooler. It can also be created artificially with aerosol spray dispensers if the humidity and temperature conditions are right."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/%D0%97%D0%B0_%D1%81%D0%B5%D0%BB%D0%BE%D0%BC_2.jpg/330px-%D0%97%D0%B0_%D1%81%D0%B5%D0%BB%D0%BE%D0%BC_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mist","wikidata":"Q192196"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/Vaccinium_fruechte_reifestadien.jpg/500px-Vaccinium_fruechte_reifestadien.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/Vaccinium_fruechte_reifestadien.jpg/1280px-Vaccinium_fruechte_reifestadien.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/Vaccinium_fruechte_reifestadien.jpg/500px-Vaccinium_fruechte_reifestadien.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Blueberries showing various stages of maturation. IG = Immature Green, GP = Green Pink, BP = Blue Pink, and R = Ripe.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AVaccinium_fruechte_reifestadien.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/Heidelbeeren_Querschnitt.jpg/500px-Heidelbeeren_Querschnitt.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/Heidelbeeren_Querschnitt.jpg/1280px-Heidelbeeren_Querschnitt.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/Heidelbeeren_Querschnitt.jpg/500px-Heidelbeeren_Querschnitt.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Whole blueberry and in cross-section","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHeidelbeeren_Querschnitt.jpg"},{"srcse